    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is additive and follows the patterns already established in this codebase:\n\n- **Cross-field rule placed at the right altitude.** Requiring \"an address in either form\" genuinely needs two fields, so a class-level `@AddressFormValidation` constraint is the correct unit \u2014 and it mirrors the existing class-level `PostcodeValidation` precedent exactly (annotation + `ConstraintValidator` pair in `rest.validation`, wired via `x-class-extra-annotation`). Dropping `@NotEmpty` from the `address` field in favor of the class-level check is the coherent consequence, while `city` keeps its field-level `@NotEmpty` since it's a single-field concern. The split field-level/class-level placement is idiomatic.\n\n- **Composition logic lives naturally in the mapper.** `composeAddress` as a `default` method invoked via a MapStruct `expression` is the standard MapStruct idiom for derived targets, and it reuses `AddressNormalizer` rather than reinventing normalization. The model additions (`addressLine1`/`addressLine2` getters/setters) are plain, conventional JavaBean accessors.\n\n- **Schema/OpenAPI edits are consistent** across all four dialects and the contract, and the composed-`address`-stored-once approach means downstream readers (`getAddress()`) transparently see the structured value without each reader needing its own fallback branch \u2014 a clean, non-duplicative design.\n\nThe only thing approaching a smell is that the \"addressLine1 present, else flat address\" precedence appears both in `composeAddress` (if/else) and `AddressFormValidator` (OR). But these sit in genuinely different layers (mapping vs. validation), the expressions aren't identical, and extracting a shared helper would couple the two concerns for little gain. Not worth a change request.",
